<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the Olympus MAIN app bundle.

  macOS "Hardened Runtime" (electron-builder `mac.hardenedRuntime: true`)
  requires every restricted operation to be declared here. Anything not listed
  is killed by the kernel at runtime — the app crashes with no useful message.
  So the rule is: add an entitlement only when a crash demands it, never
  "just in case". An over-broad entitlement set is a security liability and will
  be questioned during App Store review (if you ever ship there) or by a
  customer's own security audit.

  WHAT OLYMPUS NEEDS
  - allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      Chromium's V8 JIT-compiles code at runtime and this app is not signed
      with a secure-runtime-codec entitlement for the JIT. Without these two,
      every renderer window dies at startup. This is the single most common
      reason a hardened-runtime Electron app fails to launch.

  WHAT OLYMPUS DOES NOT NEED, and why
  - com.apple.security.device.camera / .microphone
      Olympus has no media capture. Do not add these "for the preview pane";
      the embedded preview is an <iframe> of the user's own localhost server
      and never needs hardware.
  - com.apple.security.device.location
      Olympus is a local coding harness. It has no reason to know where the
      user is.
  - com.apple.security.network.client / .network.server (as app-sandbox keys)
      These are the App Sandbox keys. Olympus is NOT sandboxed — it must spawn
      `opencode serve`, read and write arbitrary project directories, and run a
      PTY, none of which are possible inside the App Sandbox. All of Olympus'
      own network traffic goes to 127.0.0.1 on a random high port chosen at
      launch, and loopback is not restricted by any entitlement. The user's
      model servers (Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM) are likewise all on 127.0.0.1.
      There is no outbound internet traffic to declare.
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit is a sandbox key; it is only meaningful
      under the sandbox and is deliberately absent.

  Note: this file is the app's own entitlements. The helper/renderer child
  process (which loads the Chrome helpers) needs its own, slightly different
  set — see entitlementsInherit -> build/entitlemas.mac.plist.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
